WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.420
Corvian: "When the world rots, we set it to fire"

00:00:05.545 --> 00:00:08.505
"for the sake of the next world."

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.860
"It's the one thing we do right"

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.400
"Unlike those fools on the outside"

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.800
(Chuckling)

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.500
(Vaati) The Painted World is an allegory for the outside.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.620
It's a mirror we hold up, to reveal the truth.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.640
Even if things are a little backwards.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.120
Think of what this Corvian just said.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.800
(Vaati) "When the world rots, we set it afire..

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.000
for the sake of the next world"

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.600
A parallel to this is linking the fire at the end of the game

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.700
Which is very similar in concept to the fire that burns down the painting

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:51.960
The Linking of the Fire chases away the darkness just like the fire of Ariandel burns away the rot

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.680
Both worlds have this tradition

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.820
of setting themselves aflame, to rekindle a cycle of rebirth.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.500
However, consider this next line.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.980
The Corvian also say's (Vaati) "It's the one thing we do right"

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.380
"Unlike those fools on the outside"

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.480
And that's strange right?

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.120
Because last I checked, those fools on the outside they loved linking the fire.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.240
They have an entire kingdom devoted to it.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.000
So, why is this Corvian saying that they don't do it right in the outside world?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.000
That doesn't really match up.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.000
If these parallels are correct, then surely they are doing it right.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Let's try again.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.781
Perhaps rot isn't the parallel to darkness.

00:01:36.781 --> 00:01:43.800
It could make more sense, instead, for rot to be representative of the never-ending cycle of light and dark.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.500
Representative of stagnation.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:51.180
And in this comparison, the Father bleeds to satiate the flame.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.320
Just like the Unkindled is sacrificed to satiate the flame on the outside.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.300
And at the end, a new Painting is created.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.880
But this time, it's created with the Dark Soul of Man as the Pigment

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.360
Just like the first flame can be usurped by the Dark Soul of Man

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.340
Finally allowing the world to progress to the next stage

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.580
I like this set of parallels a little bit more, don't you?

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.980
Corvian: "I am so terribly frightened of timidly rotting away"

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:23.740
"Like those..."

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.140
"like those fools on the outside"

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.160
Those on the outside can be drawn into the painting and

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.960
Despair is the one thing they all have in common

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.240
One such example is the first Corvian we meet upon our arrival

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.320
Corvian: "We've all seen terrible things"

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.120
"But you're safe now"

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.020
"Let it ease your burden"

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.460
"Ariandel will make a fine home for you"

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.740
"So, go on ahead"

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.320
"Find one for yourself"

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.240
"A sweetly rotting bed to lie upon"

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.140
(Vaati): There are three types of Corvian we talk about in this video

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.940
There's the humanoid ones that look a lot like Father Ariandel

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:12.480
There's the Corvian Knights and there's the Crawling Corvians, both of which look much more crow-like

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.760
But the name "Corvian" is probably derived from the Latin word "corvus"

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.820
which means "crow" and these black feathers are what they all have in common

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.760
We get no real clue as to the origins of the humanoid Corvians

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.860
Though, they always reminded me of this quote by Nakamura in the Dark Souls I design works

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.180
where he talks about the Crows of Velka

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.860
So this developer says about the other Crows

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.020
he says: "I like to think that they were humans who wanted to fly so badly"

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.800
"that they sprouted wings but rather than their skeletons evolving over time"

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:54.220
"they instead twisted their limbs into unnatural positions, forcing their bodies into a bird-like shape"

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.580
and that's how I've always imagined them

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.260
It really reminded me of the contortions they pull to grow wings

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.780
These humanoids Corvians, they're described as having bodies, souls and weapons

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.080
that are tainted, through and through

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.180
Wretched beings with no place to go

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.340
So it makes sense that they'd find their way into the Painted World

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.840
and they rejoice upon finding a new home here

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:26.220
What sets them apart, though, is that these are the only creatures that listen to tales of the Painted World

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.500
told on the outside by their Storytellers

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.220
We know that Forlorn can be pulled into the Painted World

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.020
And we've talked about two cases of that already

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:40.720
And we know it's possible for some beings inside to leave also

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.120
So are these Corvians how the rot got into the Painting?

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.180
Or are they how the rot gets out?

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.100
Is there a deeper meaning to their Storytellers

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:55.140
who go out into the world and guide more Corvians into the Painting

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.440
by telling stories of the Painted World?

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.040
There's a few theories that could spawn from this, maybe

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.560
But, one thing is certain

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.660
They worship their Mistress of the Painted World

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.420
Friede, the first Ash to enter the Painting

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.780
and they're not the only ones

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:21.500
This is a Corvian Knight, a killing machine that is functionally identical to those Crows of Velka that you saw in Dark Souls I's Painted World

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.240
And the only piece of information that we have about those Crows of Velka

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.180
is a little excerpt from the design works of Dark Souls I

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:32.360
In a response to a question about the Crow Demons

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:38.200
Miyazaki says: "I always thought of the Painted World as somewhere where things go to escape"

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.660
"and the Bird Men are no different"

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.480
"They were originally designed as worshipers of the Goddess Velka"

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.360
"whose bodies were warped by their devotion"

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.900
I think this obsession makes them really interesting characters

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:56.760
So from the very beginning, these creatures were envisioned as having bodies warped by devotion

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:01.920
And it's impossible to know if Miyazaki still thinks of them as worshipers of Velka

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:08.240
But at the very least, we know that he, at some level, thinks of Velka as related to crows

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:15.960
and in this Painted World, the devotion of these crow-like figures is directed at another female deific form

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:16.920
Friede

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.460
Their weapons tell us that in their infatuation with Sister Friede

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:25.500
the Corvian Knights swore to protect the Painting from fire

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:29.820
And to this end, took to the execution of their own brethren

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:36.400
You saw their brethren before, they were the crows that were barely alive and they were crawling through the mud

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.320
Rotting, wandering and kept in perpetual terror by the stronger members of their former family

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.220
And it's one of the few sane members of these sad crows who tells us what happened here

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.560
Corvian: "Ohh...oh, finally"

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.140
"You have come"

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.440
"Oh wonderous ash"

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.880
"Grant us our wish"

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.080
"Make the tales true"

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:04.820
"and burn this world away"

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.620
"My lady must see flame"

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:11.600
"and you have only to show her"

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:16.500
"You are Ash, are you not?"

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.660
"Is it not fire that you seek?"

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:27.520
"Surely you've seen the rot that afflicts our world"

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.100
"But that witch fooled the good Father"

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.520
"and buried the flame"

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.300
"After we had all made up our minds, too"

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:40.680
(Vaati) We can learn a lot from these little pieces of dialogue

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.100
First, this one:

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:48.100
Corvian: "Surely you've seen the rot that afflicts our world"

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.860
"But that witch fooled the good Father"

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:54.420
"and buried the flame"

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.100
"After we had all made up our minds, too"

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:05.600
(Vaati) So this tells us that the Corvians made the decision to burn down their world for the sake of the next Painting to come

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.300
This shows that they're very aware of the nature of their own world

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.200
and they were rather central to the decision making here

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.000
Even on decent terms with the "good Father", as they call him

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.920
They blame the witch Friede for fooling Father Ariandel into burying the flame

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:30.540
But another item description says that Father Ariandel and Friede chose rot over fire together

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:36.080
So maybe she didn't fool him as much as the Corvians would like to believe

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.300
The second interesting thing is this line of dialogue:

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:42.120
Corvian: "Make the tales true"

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.920
"and burn this world away"

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:46.860
(Vaati) This line made me wonder

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.640
Who tells the tales in this world?

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.980
Storytelling is obviously a big thing

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.800
We learnt that from the Storyteller Corvians in the outside world

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:01.400
But personally, I imagine these stories coming from Father Ariandel himself

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.560
And my main bit of evidence for that is when he says:

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.680
Father Ariandel: "When the ashes are two, a flame alighteth"

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:17.160
(Vaati) Notice how there's quotation marks around this phrase and shortly afterwards he says:

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.280
Father Ariandel: "Thou'rt Ash, and fire befits thee, of course..."

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.520
(Vaati) and the quotation marks are gone

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.920
So, here, in phase three of Friede's boss fight

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:33.540
I believe that Ariandel is interpreting the tale the way he wants to

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:39.220
He's saying that Friede's black flame is the flame alighting when the ashes are two

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.920
instead of the world burning down when the ashes are two

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:49.000
So maybe he didn't come up with the tales but the tales go as far back as Father Ariandel

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.520
who was the creator of this world

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:53.180
And lastly:

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.600
"My lady must see flame"

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.580
"and you have only to show her"

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.900
The painter that we've taken to calling Aria is known to the Corvians

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:11.060
and clearly they know her as the girl who will usher in the next world, born of what happens here

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:14.120
So it makes sense then, that Friede would hide her away

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.880
It makes sense that we would talk about her next

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.560
So subscribe and I'll see you in the next one

